  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 225 PM EST Mon Nov 2 2026\n\nBroad advective forcing continues across the region with water vapor \nimagery and near term hi-res guidance initializations depicting\nnext weak short wave across the Mid MS Valley. THis should \nallow for a few more waves of drizzle and possibly a few light \nrain showers this evening but most locations will be dry or \nexperience just trace amounts. As mentioned in previous \ndiscussion, guidance has struggled today with temps with a \nmaintenance in blended guidance of a warm bias. Extensive low \nclouds and occasional very light drizzle have resulted in \ntemperatures hovering from the 55 to 60 degree range for most \nlocations. Temperatures are expected to show little movement \nthrough the night tonight as warm advection offsets most of \ndiurnal tendencies. Overnight, the aforementioned lead short \nwave should shift east of the area allowing for mid/upper level \nheight rises to build back in from the west.\n\nThe attention for Tuesday will be on evolution of large scale \nupper trough, and in particular the upper height minimum across \nthe southern Plains that will lift north as an upper jet streak \nprogresses through the downstream portion of this trough. \nMoisture transport will steadily increase in advance of this \ntrough tonight into Tuesday, but with better mid/upper dynamics \ndisplaced well west/southwest, will maintain a dry forecast \nthrough the day. Windy and warm conditions will be the main \nstory on Tuesday as near term buffer soundings suggest some \npotential of tapping into 30 to 35 knot top of boundary layer \nflow across northwest/portions of north central Indiana during \nthe afternoon hours. Thickening mid cloud and moistening low/mid\nlevels could result in mixing heights not being maximized \nhowever. Based on magnitude of low level thermals alone, highs \nshould approach record levels in the middle 70s for Tuesday.\n\nCategorical PoPs have been maintained for Tuesday night into\nWednesday AM due to the combination of initial LLJ forcing \nfollowed by passage of cold front. While cannot completely rule \nout an isolated storm with some very weak elevated instability \nTuesday night, any instability of note will likely be confined \nwell south across the Lower MS Valley. With stronger upper \ndynamics expected to bypass the area to the southwest/south, \nfrontal convergence fields are a bit weaker across the local \narea with a camp of guidance still suggesting more limited \nrainfall amounts. Given progressive nature of LLJ axis and low \nlevel baroclinic zone, this does not look to be a significant \nhydro event.\n\nDry slot punches into the area from southwest to northeast on \nWednesday, withs some additional rain showers possible \nThursday night through Friday as better moisture profiles wrap \naround the slowly eastward meandering upper low. Some lake \nenhancement is expected with the rain showers, but instability \ndepths are quite limited without any push of dramatically colder\nmid level air.\n\nThe large scale pattern will favor additional influence from an \neastern Pacific jet streak in closing off another system across\ncentral CONUS for the weekend but confidence in timing these \nadditional rain chances is low. Given expected weak \nbaroclinicity in this pattern and preference to systems more\nvertically stacked in nature, no significant rainfall events \nare anticipated through the next 7 days. Temperatures should \ntrend near normal by this weekend into early next week.",
